{
  "id": "T14_04_a_story_is_compared_under_its_interpretation_not_the_readers",
  "family": "T14",
  "rules": [
    "7.2.4",
    "7.2.6",
    "7.3.8",
    "5.5.5",
    "7.5.1",
    "4.6.1",
    "9.5.1",
    "9.4.2",
    "9.4.3"
  ],
  "description": "A normalization of the project puts the fixture fire-rating interpretation in force before Monday's issues, so Door 101's fire-rating story (\"60 min\" against \"1.5 h\") is under it throughout. The application's detection names the fixture set interpretation in its @frame: it is accepted, and its frame is recorded basis only. Wednesday's \"90 min\" equals \"1.5 h\" under the story's interpretation, so the story ends by alignment on Wednesday, citing the normalization in matched_by. A reader naming the story's interpretation sees no difference; a reader requesting literal comparison is told that the sides do not match, and the literal conflicts read reports the difference. Neither changes the story's standing.",
  "note": "7.2.4: \"A story's interpretation at an instant is the interpretation in force for its subject at that instant (7.3.8)\" and \"A detection whose `@frame` names an interpretation other than the story's is valid, and its frame is recorded basis only (7.5.1). A read under a different interpretation reports, alongside the story, whether its sides match under that interpretation, without changing the story's standing.\" 7.2.6(a): \"Where they are equal only under an interpretation that a normalization put in force, the ending also cites that normalization (5.5.4).\" The story's interpretation is put in force by a normalization, not named only by a detection's frame.",
